  urldate = {2020-12-23},
  abstract = {Buildings account for 32\% of the energy use worldwide. A new regime of exciting new ``applications'' that span a distributed fabric of sensors, actuators and humans have emerged to improve building energy efficiency and operations management. These applications leverage the technological advances in embedded sensing, processing, networking and methods by which they can be coupled with supervisory control and data acquisition systems deployed in modern buildings and with users on mobile wireless platforms. There are, however, several technical challenges before such a vision of smart building applications and cyber-physical systems can be realized. The sensory data from and to the distributed systems end-points need significant curation before it can be used meaningfully. This is largely a manual, cost-prohibitive task and hence such solutions rarely experience widespread adoption due to the lack of a common descriptive schema.},
