  doi = {10/ggd8nn},
  urldate = {2019-12-09},
  abstract = {Interactive ray tracing applications running on commodity hardware can su er from objectionable temporal artifacts due to a low sample count. We introduce stable ray tracing, a technique that improves temporal stability without the over-blurring and ghosting artifacts typical of temporal post-processing lters. Our technique is based on sample reprojection and explicit hole lling, rather than relying on hole- lling heuristics that can compromise image quality. We make reprojection practical in an interactive ray tracing context through the use of a super-resolution bitmask to estimate screen space sample density. We show signi cantly improved temporal stability as compared with supersampling and an existing reprojection techniques. We also investigate the performance and image quality di erences between our technique and temporal antialiasing, which typically incurs a signi cant amount of blur. Finally, we demonstrate the bene ts of stable ray tracing by combining it with progressive path tracing of indirect illumination.},
