  abstract = {We report the results of a large-scale ophthalmic screening effort to characterize how blindness among children enrolled in Indian schools for the blind distributes across different ocular sites. The ocular locus information constrains the etiology of blindness and the feasibility of treatment. Specifically, we examined 2,336 children across 27 schools for the blind in six north Indian states, determined the affected ocular loci, and analyzed the data along the dimensions of age, gender, parental education, and geographical location. We find that whole globe and retinal pathologies are predominant causes of blindness in our sample. However, incidence of anterior segment related blindness is still high, considering the treatability of these conditions in many cases. Comparing our data across age groups (which span over 15 years) it is apparent that the distribution of anatomical causes has stayed largely unchanged across the cross-sectional sample, suggesting relative constancy in underlying causal factors. We discuss the policy implications of these findings.},
